d gratitude after a performance.\n\n**3. Is this at the beginning or end of a performance?**\nThis is unequivocally at the **end** of a performance. The sequence of actions—a deep bow, a wide gesture of thanks, a celebratory raise of hands, and a final wave—is the standard protocol for figure skaters to acknowledge the audience and judges after completing their routine. The presence of the Olympic branding and the crowd\'s visible engagement further confirm this is a post-performance moment.\n\n**4. Describe their exact actions and body language.**\n*   **Tessa Virtue (Woman):** She begins with a deep, respectful bow, showing humility and gratitude. She then stands tall, extends her arm gracefully, and raises her hand in celebration. Her final pose is one of happiness and connection with her partner, as she turns towards him while waving to the crowd. Her body language is elegant, expressive, and joyful.\n*   **Scott Moir (Man):** He starts by standing tall and observing his partner\'s bow, showing support. He then mirrors her gesture of gratitude by extending his arm. He raises his hand high in a powerful, celebratory gesture, sharing in the triumph. In the final frames, he places a hand on her shoulder in a gesture of affection and partnership, smiling warmly at her and the audience. His body language is strong, supportive, and celebratory.\n\nIn summary, the frames capture Tessa Virtue and Scott Moir at the conclusion of their ice dance performance at the **Vancouver 2010 Winter Olympics**. They are performing a series of bows and gestures to thank the audience, displaying a mix of humility, triumph, and deep partnership.'}], 'formatted_responses': ['\n=== Batch 0-5 (5 frames) ===\nFrames: /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2LH3JCGkEBU/frames/frame_0539.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2LH3JCGkEBU/frames/frame_0538.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2LH3JCGkEBU/frames/frame_0540.jpg, /mnt/ssd/data/lvbench/videos_processed/2LH3JCGkEBU/frame